**Claim 1.** A variable compression ratio system for a reciprocating piston engine, comprising:

a crankshaft rotatably mounted within an engine block and coupled to at least one piston via a multi-link connecting assembly, said assembly including an eccentric control member operable to vary an effective top-dead-center position of the piston and thereby alter a compression ratio of a combustion chamber;

a plurality of magnets fixed to the crankshaft at predetermined angular positions thereon;

one or more induction devices disposed proximate a rotational path of said magnets and configured to generate an electrical signal in response to passage of said magnets thereby, said signal being indicative of at least one of a rotational position, a rotational speed, and an angular acceleration of the crankshaft;

a hydraulic actuator operatively engaged with said eccentric control member and arranged to displace said control member to a plurality of positions corresponding to respective compression ratios;

a control valve in fluid communication with said hydraulic actuator and configured to regulate a flow of pressurized fluid thereto so as to selectively drive said actuator; and

a controller in electrical communication with said one or more induction devices and with said control valve, said controller being configured to receive said signal from said induction devices and, in dependence thereon, to command actuation of said control valve so as to adjust the position of said eccentric control member and establish a target compression ratio corresponding to a sensed operating condition of the engine.

---

**Plain-language note:** In practice, the *induction devices and crankshaft magnets* form a position/speed sensor; the *control valve* meters hydraulic pressure to an *actuator*; and the actuator moves a *linkage or eccentric* that raises or lowers how far the piston travels, thereby squeezing the fuel-air charge more (high compression, efficiency) or less (low compression, allowing higher boost without knock). The dependent claims below would typically narrow specifics.

